# Seats and keys

- **Seat**: One run's or one device's place in one room, its **seat kind** `run`
  or `device`, named by its seat certificate, made when the seat starts
  (PRV-11), the only source ParleyInn shows a seat's kind from; a seat key minted on
  the loss of a run seat's key with its MCP server⟦f:node-clone⟧, or on a node identity change
  or a node clone⟦/f⟧⟦f:store-protection⟧, or on a backup restore⟦/f⟧⟦f:key-revocation⟧, or on the revocation of one of this
  node's own device seats' keys or of a continuing run's run-seat key⟦/f⟧ starts
  another (Seat key). The unit of membership, signing and authorship. Each seat
  of the personal room's principal in the personal room⟦f:paired-phone⟧, a paired phone's device
  seat in the personal room of the node it pairs with included,⟦/f⟧ is a member there
  from its first event⟦f:paired-phone⟧, or its pairing,⟦/f⟧ with no add, but the device seat whose
  create room act made the room; every other seat keeps its place while its
  **add** stands: the act that placed it, its create room act⟦f:work-rooms⟧, a join its
  principal asked for or accepted⟦f:shared-rooms⟧⟦f:principal-keys⟧ that the room's admission admitted⟦/f⟧⟦/f⟧, a
  subagent run's join under its parent run's join or create room act (Subagent)⟦f:access-tokens⟧, a join its access
  token names (Join)⟦/f⟧, or a device seat's join⟦f:shared-rooms⟧⟦f:principal-keys⟧ without admission⟦/f⟧⟦/f⟧⟦/f⟧; the seat
  ingest starts keeps its place while the run seat it names does, sharing that
  seat's add, one for both, where it has one (LANE-25, REC-19).
- **Run seat**: A run's seat. A witnessed run's run-seat key, but for the seat
  ingest starts, lives only in the memory of its harness session's MCP server
  once that server holds it, which keeps the run-seat keys of the main run and
  its subagents' runs and seals their writers (SEC-10); until then the core
  mints and keeps it like a device seat's key and seals its writer, and a key
  lost with its MCP server starts a new seat that replaces and names the old one
  (Seat key);
  what `parinn ingest` appends, whether or not that
  server still runs, goes to the one seat and writer ingest starts beside that
  run seat, which every later `parinn ingest` extends and the core seals, naming
  the run seat, in the same room, sharing its add, where it has one, and taking
  over its role⟦f:work-rooms⟧, so a ⟦f:moderation⟧kick or ⟦/f⟧leave of either seat ends that one add for both⟦/f⟧
  (REC-19); that seat is a run seat whose key the core keeps like a device
  seat's key; an ingested run's seat key is kept like a device seat's key, and
  the core seals its writer.
- **Device seat**: A principal's seat for one device, a node⟦f:paired-phone⟧ or a paired phone⟦/f⟧.
  The one seat kind for acting without a run. It takes room acts only at a
  principal surface, but for the create room act of its node's personal room
  (Personal room)⟦f:work-rooms⟧ and the joins its node takes (Join)⟦/f⟧⟦f:facilitator⟧, and for the facilitator's
  device seat, through its program's CLI calls (Facilitator)⟦/f⟧; an agent's tool
  call acts only through its run seat.⟦f:access-tokens⟧ A token-key-only node's device
  seat is certified by its token key, and that node signs no principal⟦f:principal-keys⟧ or expire⟦/f⟧
  acts.⟦/f⟧
- **Room id**: 128 random bits its create room act fixes, never chosen, bound
  to the key that certified the creating seat (LANE-01); a create room act under
  any other certifying key that names it is refused and counted (I6).
- **Seat id**: A seat's id, derived from the room id and the seat's first key.
  Nobody chooses it.
- **Seat key**: A seat's one current key. It signs the seat's room acts and
  seals its writer. A rotation, signed by the old and the new key, keeps the
  seat's id and writer and goes to that seat's own writer (SEC-27). A key minted
  because a run seat's key was lost with its MCP server (Run seat)⟦f:node-clone⟧, or on a node
  identity change or a node clone (REC-24⟦f:store-protection⟧, ADM-06⟦/f⟧)⟦/f⟧⟦f:store-protection⟧, or on a backup restore
  (Backup)⟦/f⟧ starts a new seat and writer that replaces and names the old one⟦f:node-clone⟧,
  taking it as its predecessor only as Node says⟦/f⟧, and inherits no add⟦f:moderation⟧, no appointment⟦/f⟧ and no role⟦f:work-rooms⟧: outside the personal room it joins as any seat does
  (LANE-23), and roles⟦f:moderation⟧ and appointments⟦/f⟧ are assigned again⟦/f⟧; a personal-room seat
  is a member from its first event. The seat ingest starts shares the add, where
  it has one, and takes over the role instead (Run seat).⟦f:key-revocation⟧ Revoking a seat key
  ends it: the revocation names the seat's last accepted seq, the seat's acts
  and seals past it count for nothing, whatever their causal order, and the pins
  and pin versions the seat wrote stop qualifying (Pin), so revoking a device
  seat's key is widening and a run seat's is cut⟦f:stamps⟧, unless it stops a version a principal
  stamped from qualifying, which makes it widening as any act removing a pin
  from a restore block is⟦/f⟧ (Principal act). Revoking the key of one of this node's
  own current device seats mints a new key, certified by the node's device key,
  that starts a new device seat and writer in its room as above, naming the
  revoked seat⟦f:node-clone⟧ without taking it as its predecessor, and taking the revoked
  seat's own predecessors, if any, as its predecessors⟦/f⟧. A device seat whose key is
  revoked no longer counts as one of this node's own device seats⟦f:node-clone⟧, and only the
  predecessors of its current device seats count as theirs⟦/f⟧. Revoking a run
  seat's key while its run continues mints a new key that starts a new run seat
  and writer in its room as above, replacing and naming the revoked seat, and
  the run's later events go there.⟦/f⟧
- **Device key**: A device's key⟦f:principal-keys⟧, certified by a principal key's **device
  certificate**, with a **device scope** (the kinds of principal act it may
  sign, and of⟦f:conversation⟧ post and⟦/f⟧ pin its seats may write)⟦f:rule-levels⟧ and a maximum rule level⟦/f⟧⟦/f⟧; it
  certifies the seats its device uses (Device)⟦f:principal-keys⟧. It
  signs principal acts and expire acts once PRV-10 ships⟦/f⟧⟦f:git-carrier⟧, and a room's segments
  the git carrier carries⟦f:blind-peer⟧⟦f:peer-sync⟧ or a blind peer holds⟦/f⟧⟦/f⟧ are encrypted to its principals'
  device keys⟦f:access-tokens⟧, or a token-key-only node's token key⟦/f⟧ (PEER-08⟦f:blind-peer⟧, PEER-12⟦/f⟧)⟦/f⟧⟦f:principal-keys⟧. Before
  PRV-10 ships no principal key certifies a device key, so its device scope is
  every kind of principal act⟦f:conversation⟧, post⟦/f⟧ and pin⟦f:rule-levels⟧, with no maximum rule level⟦/f⟧, and a
  device seat's pins qualify only on its own node⟦f:node-clone⟧ and on nodes whose device
  seats have it among their predecessors, however far back⟦/f⟧ (Pin)⟦/f⟧.
⟦f:principal-keys⟧- **Principal key**: A principal's root key, kept offline or in a
  platform-protected key store and never kept by a ParleyInn component (the
  principal's own key tooling signs with it), which certifies its device keys⟦f:service-accounts⟧
  and may certify a service account's principal key by a **service-account
  certificate**, which marks whether that account relays text others wrote⟦/f⟧
  (PRV-10).⟦/f⟧
⟦f:access-tokens⟧- **Token key**: A key a device key certifies by a **token certificate**, which
  may stand between a device key and a seat key for an ephemeral node, limited
  to an access token's rooms, the rooms it may create, its node's own personal
  room and its expiry (PRV-10); none exists before PRV-10 ships, and so no
  token-key-only node does. A node with only a token key signs no principal⟦f:principal-keys⟧
  or expire⟦/f⟧ acts; ⟦f:stamps⟧every pin it writes but a configuration pin (Configuration
  pin) restores only once a principal stamps it from one of its devices whose
  device scope allows it, though ⟦/f⟧its own events are trusted on that node as
  that node's trusted sources.⟦/f⟧
- **Seat certificate**: A device key's⟦f:access-tokens⟧ or token key's⟦/f⟧ signature over a seat key,
  scoped to the seat's room, naming the seat kind, `run` or `device`. The node's
  device key makes one for every seat the node uses from the first release
  (PRV-11)⟦f:principal-keys⟧; once
  PRV-10 ships every seat key chains to a principal key⟦/f⟧. A node's **key set** is
  the keys and certificates it holds and the recorded acts ending any of them,
  such as a rotation (I10).
⟦f:access-tokens⟧- **Access token**: A short-lived credential a principal mints to enroll a
  device⟦f:peer-sync⟧ or peer⟦/f⟧, certify an ephemeral node's seats⟦f:shared-rooms⟧⟦f:principal-keys⟧ or carry an invite link⟦/f⟧⟦/f⟧
  (PEER-06, PRV-10⟦f:shared-rooms⟧⟦f:principal-keys⟧, LANE-18⟦/f⟧⟦/f⟧). "Token" is always an access token, a model token,
  an access token's token key, a token certificate or a token-key-only node.⟦/f⟧
⟦f:presence-proof⟧- **Authenticator**: A hardware-backed key that gives presence proofs (OWN-11).⟦/f⟧
⟦f:ci-attestation⟧- **CI key**: A key the room's owner enrolled in the room to sign CI
  attestations.⟦/f⟧
